# pnpm settings for this project. Written once when absent, then yours.
#
# This file is NOT about workspaces, despite its name. pnpm 10 reads its
# settings from here, and a single application with no `packages:` key is a
# supported shape — `create-next-app` writes the same file for a single app.
#
# Why it ships: pnpm 10 refuses a dependency's install scripts by default.
# `pnpm install` prints "Ignored build scripts: ..." and still exits 0, so a
# package that needs to build a native binary never builds one while every
# static gate stays green. The failure then surfaces at the first real
# request — not at install, type-check, unit-test or build time.
#
# `onlyBuiltDependencies` is the allowlist. pnpm also reads it from
# package.json's `pnpm` field; this file keeps it out of the manifest the
# project edits by hand.
#
# Measured on pnpm 10.15.0, with a control:
#   - This file IS read in a single-app project. The control run, identical
#     but with the file removed, ignored the scripts even under
#     `pnpm install --force`.
#   - A first install (no `node_modules/`) runs the approved scripts under a
#     plain `pnpm install`.
#   - Adding a name to a tree that is ALREADY installed is not enough on its
#     own: a plain `pnpm install` is a no-op that repeats the same warning.
#     Use `pnpm install --force`, or `pnpm rebuild <pkg>`.
#   - A name for a package this project does not depend on is inert: no
#     warning, no error.
#
# `pnpm approve-builds` edits this list for you, interactively.
#
# pnpm 11 replaces `onlyBuiltDependencies` with `allowBuilds` (a map, not a
# list). This project pins pnpm 10, so the list form is correct here. Change
# the key when you move the pin, not before.

onlyBuiltDependencies:
  # Required by the SQLite stack the prisma block documents. Each entry is
  # inert when that block is not inlined, so the default is safe either way.
  - "@prisma/engines"
  - better-sqlite3
  - prisma
